In the area of learning, for example, the acquisition of basic academic skills and their transfer to other situations represents a central educational objective, whereas students in the area of intellectual development may require a more development-oriented form (erklärung was das ist) of support that addresses fundamental cognitive competencies. At the same time, students within each of the three areas do not form a homogeneous group and therefore require individually adapted support. This is particularly evident in autism spectrum disorder, as each child presents a unique combination of characteristics. The disorder may occur in conjunction with severe cognitive impairments, but also with high cognitive abilities.
This heterogeneity therefore requires an inclusive educational approach that builds on the individual prerequisites of each child and provides appropriately adapted learning opportunities and support measures (quelle).

Despite these differences, several common principles for the design of suitable learning opportunities can be derived across the three areas: (quelle?)
- Individual adaptation rather than standardised requirements
- Clear and manageable task structures
- Visualisation of processes and sequences of actions
- Sufficient processing time and opportunities for repetition
- Playful and action-oriented tasks
- Timely and comprehensible feedback
- Promotion of independence and self-efficacy

One way of addressing different learning prerequisites is to make learning content accessible through different perceptual and action-based approaches. In this context, the interaction between motor and perceptual experience plays a particularly important role, since learners can develop sensorimotor integration processes and support learning at an elementary level through active engagement and the conscious perception of their own movements and environment. (Kultusministerkonferenz, 2021).

A suitable framework for such action-oriented experiences provide playful and constructive forms of learning, as they offer opportunities to develop and strengthen sensory, motor, and cognitive abilities within concrete contexts of action. At the same time, they enable exploratory experimentation in which errors are accepted as part of the learning process (Kultusministerkonferenz, 2021).

In addition to designing appropriate learning approaches, the promotion of self-directed learning represents another important educational objective intended to strengthen students’ independence. However, the ability to complete a task independently requires learners to recognise the effects of their actions and adapt their subsequent approach accordingly. Timely and comprehensible feedback is therefore essential, because otherwise there will be a risk that incorrect procedures are continued unnoticed or uncertainty arise regarding how to proceed with the task. In heterogeneous or inclusive classrooms, however, a teacher cannot continuously observe every action of every student and provide immediate feedback. Particularly during phases of independent practice, a delay may therefore occur between an action and the corresponding feedback. For learners who depend on clear confirmation or correction in order to continue a task, this can make independent task completion more difficult. (quelle?)


=== Existing learning materials 
Construction materials such as building blocks are generally well suited to action-oriented and self-directed forms of learning because they combine concrete actions with the exploration of spatial and logical relationships. Through active construction, experimentation, and problem-solving, children can engage with learning content in a playful manner. At the same time, the result remains visible as a physical artefact and can be directly examined and modified.However, constrcution materials are generally unable to independently detect or evaluate the resulting construction. The feedback required for self-directed learning must therefore be provided either by the learners themselves or by a teacher (quelle Steffie?).

At the same time, digital technologies such as tablets make it possible to present learning content through multimedia, adapt tasks to individual needs, and provide automated feedback. However, spatial content is often represented only on a two-dimensional screen, while interaction is largely limited to mouse or touch input. Although these forms of input also enable active interaction, they do not provide the same haptic and spatially differentiated experience as the manipulation of three-dimensional objects. This reduces the physical and motor interaction with learning objects, which is highly relevant for successful learning, to a minimum (quelle Steffie?).

== Objective of the Thesis
Against this background, this thesis develops a modular, brick-based learning game that combines the haptic and spatial properties of conventional construction materials with the possibilities of digital detection, visualisation, and feedback. Within a guided construction task, children can use the bricks to build a three-dimensional structure and receive immediate feedback on whether their construction corresponds to the target structure or contains errors. Feedback is provided both directly through the bricks and through an additional visualization on a display. The power is thereby supplied centrally from the baseplate and distributed through all the connected bricks

The physical manipulation of the bricks combines visual, haptic, and motor experiences, while the digital representation provides additional information about the detected state of the construction. Through immediate feedback, the learning game is intended to enable learners to independently monitor and correct individual action steps. In this way, it aims to support self-directed learning, action planning, spatial and relational thinking, and problem-solving.

